  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 100 AM MDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nEnsemble guidance remains in good agreement on the synoptic \nevolution through late week and into next weekend, featuring a \nlate summer Southern High Plains/Central Rockies ridge of high \npressure pattern. The Tri-State Area will sit on the northern \nperiphery of the mid level ridge axis, maintaining generally \nzonal flow aloft with subtle shortwave trough impulses ejecting \nout of Colorado on Thursday and Friday afternoons. \n\nHigh confidence exists regarding near to above normal warmth. \nThe NBM 25th to 75th percentile range for daily maximum \ntemperatures remains exceptionally tight spread of only 3-5 \ndegrees for Saturday and Sunday with temps nearing 100, \nindicating strong ensemble consensus for above normal \ntemperatures this weekend. Model spread is higher i.e. higher \nuncertainty for maximum temperatures for Thursday and Friday on \nthe order of 6-10 degrees ranging from the upper 80s to the \nlower 90s depending on cloud cover and precipitation coverage. \nAs the 500 mb ridge amplifies slightly over the Southern Plains \nFriday into Sunday, warm 850 mb thermal ridges 30-33 C build \ninto the Plains. Median NBM highs push into the upper 90s for \nmost of the CWA, with the eastern third e.g Hill City, Norton, \nMcCook areas seeing a 40-60% probability of temperatures topping\n100 degrees on Saturday and 60-80% on Sunday for much of the \narea. Maximum afternoon heat index values across eastern \nlocations may approach between 100 and 105 on Saturday and \nSunday afternoons as low level moisture lingers east of the \ndryline. \n\nThursday Afternoon PoPs peak in the 20-40% range west of KS Hwy\n27 and across eastern Colorado Cheyenne/Kit Carson counties \nwhere terrain forced lift and low-level convergence are \ngreatest. Later in the evening model forecasts indicate higher \nprobabilities heading into Thursday night with increasing \nchances of at least some precipitation with model averages \nbetween 0.15 and 0.25, locally higher amounts possible as high \nas 40% chance of exceeding 0.30. Ensemble SBCAPE probabilities \nexceed 40% for > 1000 J/kg east of the dryline each afternoon. \nHowever, deep-layer shear remains modest 20-25 kts, keeping \nstorms mostly somewhat unorganized at this time. High inverted-V\nlow levels DCAPE > 1200 J/kg suggest localized strong downburst\nwinds 40-60 mph will be the primary threats with collapsing \ncells.",
